I most definitely am judging; the death drop has become a misfeature, and
it greatly exacerbates the consequences of pudding farming, which is bad
and wrong - and this decade's "quit every time you establish smoky potions
are not object detection".

It is, I believe, a fundamental design criterion that the game must remain
challenging; that there should be no trivially and indefinitely exploitable
technique for gaining loot. The "why prevent people from doing whatever is
fun" argument is fatally flawed in two respects.

Firstly, this is necessary if the multiplayer aspects - meaningfully
comparable scores (well, for non-ascensions); knowing that ascension in
and of itself is always a feat to be proud of; bones files, etc. - are to
remain sensible. 

Secondly, for those of us who do wish to play a challenging game, the game
is weakened by that attitude; ultimately all these indefinitely repeatable
techniques stem from the fact that there's no clock at all on the middle
game, no reason not to engage in indefinite farming.

[Such a clock need not make the game harder for genuinely novice players,
who cannot think of nearly as many ways to improve the character in the
middle game as an experienced player can.]

Likewise a limit on HP and protection gain from prayer would cut out a lot
of farming's benefit, without affecting ordinary play; but it would cut
the effect of an exceptional run of luck.
